 ## Overview
-Ray Marching with Neural SDF is a rendering technique that approximates the Space Distance Field (SDF) using a deep neural network and renders images by marching rays along the estimated distance field [S1, unofficial]. It replaces explicit geometric primitives or voxel grids with a learned implicit representation, allowing for the rendering of complex, continuous shapes defined by the network's latent code [S1, unofficial]. Unlike traditional sphere tracing which relies on analytical SDFs, this method evaluates a neural network at each step, trading computational speed for geometric flexibility and compact storage [S1, unofficial].
+Ray Marching with Neural SDF is a rendering technique that approximates the Space Distance Field (SDF) using a deep neural network and renders images by marching rays along the estimated distance field [S1, unofficial]. It substitutes the analytical distance query $f(\mathbf{p})$ with a neural network inference $N(\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{p})$, where $\mathbf{z}$ is a latent code representing the shape and $\mathbf{p}$ is the 3D query point [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on [Distance-field ray marching](21-distance-field-ray-marching.md) by replacing explicit geometric primitives with a learned implicit representation [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
